  "problem": "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i?$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\overset{\u2015}{z}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i,$ the imaginary part is $-\\sqrt{2}\\,i$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $\\sqrt{2}\\,i.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{\u2015}{z}=\\sqrt{2}\\,i.$",
